In Java Swing, `setDefaultCloseOperation` is a method used to define what happens when the user closes a window. The most common options are:

1. `JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE` - Exit the application when the window is closed
2. `JFrame.HIDE_ON_CLOSE` - Hide the window but keep the application running
3. `JFrame.DISPOSE_ON_CLOSE` - Dispose of the window but keep the application running
4. `JFrame.DO_NOTHING_ON_CLOSE` - Disable the close operation

Here's a complete example:

```java
import javax.swing.JFrame;

public class MainWindow extends JFrame {
    public MainWindow() {
        // Set the title of the window
        setTitle("My Application");

        // Set the size of the window
        setSize(800, 600);

        // Set what happens when the window is closed
        setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        // Make the window visible
        setVisible(true);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new MainWindow();
    }
}
```

