## Understanding Smart Pointers

Smart pointers in C++ are wrapper classes around raw pointers that help manage the lifetime of dynamically allocated objects. They ensure that the object is properly destroyed (and its memory deallocated) when it is no longer needed, preventing memory leaks.

The two most commonly used smart pointers are `std::unique_ptr` and `std::shared_ptr`. The key difference lies in **ownership semantics**—how ownership of the managed object is represented and transferred.

---

### `std::unique_ptr<T>` — Exclusive Ownership

`std::unique_ptr` represents **exclusive ownership** of a dynamically allocated object. This means:

- **Only one** `unique_ptr` can own the object at any given time.
- The object is destroyed when the `unique_ptr` goes out of scope or is explicitly reset.
- **Copy construction and copy assignment are deleted** — you cannot copy a `unique_ptr` because that would imply two pointers owning the same object, violating exclusive ownership.
- **Move construction and move assignment are allowed** — you can transfer ownership from one `unique_ptr` to another.

#### Example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass() { std::cout << "MyClass constructed\n"; }
    ~MyClass() { std::cout << "MyClass destroyed\n"; }
    void greet() { std::cout << "Hello from MyClass!\n"; }
};

int main() {
    // Create a unique_ptr
    std::unique_ptr<MyClass> ptr1 = std::make_unique<MyClass>();

    ptr1->greet();  // Use the object

    // std::unique_ptr<MyClass> ptr2 = ptr1;  // ERROR: copy is deleted

    // Transfer ownership using move
    std::unique_ptr<MyClass> ptr2 = std::move(ptr1);

    // ptr1 is now nullptr
    if (!ptr1) {
        std::cout << "ptr1 is now null\n";
    }

    ptr2->greet();

    return 0;  // ptr2 goes out of scope, MyClass is destroyed
}
```

